        </solution>
        </task>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="0.5in">
        <p>
            Write an expression for the sum of three consecutive numbers.
        </p>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <md>(n)+(n+1)+(n+2)</md>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
        <p>
            Write an expression for the product of three consecutive even numbers.
        </p>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <md>
                    (n)(n+2)(n+4)
                </md>
                
            </p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
        <p>
            Generalize the solution to this polygon puzzle by writing the upper left corner as <m>p</m>
            and then writing the remaining vertices in terms of <m>p</m>.
        </p>
        <image source="puzzle1.png" width="35%">
            <shortdescription>Square polygon puzzle with sides 8, 14, 12, and 6 moving clockwise around the square. </shortdescription>
        </image>
        <solution>
            <p>
                To find the upper right vertex we subtract <m>p</m> from 8.  
                To find the lower left vertex we subtract <m>p</m> from 6.
                To find the lower right vertex we can subtract <m>8-p</m> from 14.
                <md>
                    14-(8-p)
                </md>
                <md>
                    14-8-(-p)
                </md>
                <md>
                    6+p
                </md>
                To find the lower right vertex we could also have subtracted <m>6-p</m> from 12.
                <md>12-(6-p)</md>
                <md>12-6-(-p)</md>
                <md>6+p</md>
            </p>
            <image source="puzzle1s.png" width="35%">
            <shortdescription>Square polygon puzzle with sides 8, 14, 12, and 6 moving clockwise around the square. </shortdescription>
        </image>
        </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
        <p>
            Generalize the solution to this polygon puzzle by writing the upper left corner as <m>p</m>
            and then writing the remaining vertices in terms of <m>p</m>.
        </p>
        <image source="puzzle2.png" width="35%">
            <shortdescription>Square polygon puzzle with sides 10, 9, 11, and 12 moving clockwise around the square. </shortdescription>
        </image>
        <solution>
            <p>
                To find the upper right vertex we subtract <m>p</m> from 10.  
                To find the lower left vertex we subtract <m>p</m> from 12.
                To find the lower right vertex we can subtract <m>10-p</m> from 9.
                <md>
                    9-(10-p)
                </md>
                <md>
                    9-10-(-p)
                </md>
                <md>
                    -1+p
                </md>
                To find the lower right vertex we could also have subtracted <m>12-p</m> from 11.
                <md>11-(12-p)</md>
                <md>11-12-(-p)</md>
                <md>-1+p</md>
            </p>
            <image source="puzzle2s.png" width="35%">
            <shortdescription>Square polygon puzzle with sides 10, 9, 11, and 12 moving clockwise around the square. </shortdescription>
        </image>
        </solution>
    </exercise>
</worksheet>